at e3 a few weeks ago Todd Howard took  the stage to talk about fallout 76 the  game had been announced one week earlier  with a brief teaser trailer set to John  Denver's crooning tones but but that's  days press conference offered actual  concrete details we now know 76 will be  focused on multiplayer survival we know  it will be set in West Virginia and we  know it will emphasize settlement  building a system Bethesda Game Studios  established in 2015's fallout 4 as I  watched Todd promote his team's game and  marveled at the craftsmanship of that  sick leather jacket something occurred  to me I had only ever put 20 hours into  fallout 4 as with any of bgs is  open-world titles this is only a drop in  a massive bucket I hadn't reached the  level cap or played any of the  expansions or even completed the main  storyline  so I've considered myself a fallout fan  for a long time fallout 3 is one of my  favorite games and I absolutely adore  what obsidian did with Fallout New Vegas  I even went back to the first two titles  which have an age that well to see what  interplay in black I'll did to get the  series off the ground but in 2015 I  bounced off of fallout 4 maybe it was  the packed schedule of fall releases or  the numerous other games I was reviewing  for work purposes but for whatever  reason I spent a few fleeting days with  fallout 4 and quickly moved on so now  with all the excitement surrounding the  upcoming fallout entry and a brief lull  in this year's major titles I decided to  retrace my footsteps to give fallout 4  another shot to not only see what I've  been missing out on but to investigate  why such a huge entry in one of my  favorite series failed to grab me almost  three years ago  [Music]  only one hour into my new play through i  remember just how much fallout4  overwhelmed me in the first place after  emerging from vault 111 returning to my  home in sanctuary hills and fighting  through Concord with the Minutemen I  rediscovered the game's crafting system  this includes everything from modular  weapon upgrades to numerous armor  combinations perk requirements to outfit  enhancements even power armors relegated  to its own crafting tree and drastically  changes combat when you Don it as  someone who wore Fallout 3's t51 B  Armour religiously it took me a while to  think of the exosuits more as vehicles  than just outfits and of course none of  this is to mention fallout 4 is divisive  workshops if one feature serves as a  microcosm for Fallout 4 it's this a  system that allows you to construct and  modify your own wasteland outposts  complete with resources to manage and  citizens to care for you not only build  your own house but an entire town if you  devote time to creating numerous  settlements they'll eventually spread  throughout the Commonwealth three years  ago these elements were a major  deterrent for me they felt like  minecraft Lite tacked onto a game that  didn't need it to me the fallout world  has always been about wandering  scavenging living the life of a nomad in  a dangerous American hellscape to settle  down meant to wither to focus on  specific plots of land meant to ignore  the very freedom that made Bethesda's  open-world so appealing in the first  place but replaying fallout 4 in the  wake of community mods workshop DLC  packs and an array of amazing YouTube  videos about to give the settlement  aspect a little more time and in doing  so I fell in love with it the controls  and UI took a while to master especially  considering I was playing on ps4 as I  owned all of the DLC on this platform  but once I got the hang of menu  navigation there was no looking back I  bolstered my defenses I restored power  to my living quarters I even set up a  quaint little diner where greatful  patrons can relax after a hard day's  work rebuilding these camps encourages  creativity on its own but also does a  stellar job of feeding into the rest of  the game in order to create you have to  scavenge in order to scavenge you have  to explore and while exploring a  dangerous wasteland you'll likely get  into a firefight or two which then  yields more material  for crafting and that brings you right  back to your hideouts to store your best  loot fallout 4 settlements are the glue  that holds these various mechanics  together and I can't imagine the game  without them it's good to see artillery  here again to dig a little deeper this  focus on post-apocalyptic domestication  also supports Fallout 4s narrative while  earlier Fallout games told stories of  reconstruction but as does newest entry  places the idea at the heart of things  in fact most of the games role-playing  which I'll get to shortly centers around  one major question what do you want the  wasteland to be for instance you can  rebuild the Minutemen the wastelands  protective militia you liberate farms  and defend drive-in theaters you  increase the group's influence over the  Commonwealth until you retake the old  fortress itself reinforcing the castle  with artillery storefronts and crops  alternatively you could aid the railroad  a covert organization that smuggles  synths to freedom away from the  totalitarian aims of the corporate  Institute as with the Minutemen you  establish settlements in order to expand  the railroad network by constructing  outposts you actively grow the group's  reach as you might imagine I was  borderline shocked to learn that the  workshops were almost cut during  development several times  according to no clips history of  bethesda video the team saw it as  ancillary to the overall experience an  ambitious endeavor that could have  easily been abandoned I'm glad it wasn't  it fosters my attachment to the  Commonwealth wasteland serving a  narrative purpose but also giving me  opportunities to put my own mark on the  surroundings fallout 4s DLC doubled down  on the workshops not only other upgrades  to your furniture structures and  defenses but also a whole story based  expansion in Nuka world which allows you  to help Raider gangs in their bid for  dominance the DLC lets you build a  Raider Empire complete with its own  settlements and hideouts it would have  made more sense to include this in the  base game fleshing out another faction  in Fallout 4s role-playing framework but  nevertheless illustrates Bethesda's  confidence in their crafting system  really my altered perception of the  workshop system reflects my newfound  appreciation for Fallout 4 as a whole  looking back on it three years later  allows me to place it in a new context  with more details about fallout 76  I can see fallout 4 as part of a gradual  evolution that spans multiple developers  interplay and black isle may have began  the series as role-playing games but  since the 2008 release of Fallout 3  they've become something different  open-world shooters with RPG elements  that's not to say you can't really role  play in these games there are always  ample opportunities to create a  character and stick to that persona new  vegas in particular encourages you to  build a framework of morals and  motivations and pushes you through its  narrative with somewhat difficult  choices but by and large bethesda has  pushed role-playing to the background  making more room for explorations  survival and combat in the process  following the series from fallout 3 to  the upcoming fallout 76 you can see the  developer increasingly focus on these  three areas they improve crafting and  combat drastically in Fallout 4 they  subsequently added survival mode to make  the game more difficult  almost every upgradable perk is geared  toward fighting or crafting and with  fallout 76 their marketing is stressing  survival crafting and exploration over  anything else any mentions of  role-playing seem to arise as an  afterthought placed in this evolutionary  context I think fallout 4 is much much  better than I originally gave it credit  for  yes it's rife with bugs and on consoles  the framerate drops low enough to still  warrant a mention three years and  numerous updates later  there are also myriad problems when the  narrative and I'm about to get to those  but consider 76 is increased focus on  multiplayer and survival by all accounts  Bethesda should be running a huge risk  of alienating long-term players such as  myself but not only does fallout 4  convinced me that fallout 76 can fit  logically into the series evolution it  actively has me excited for it and I say  this as someone who puts past Fallout  games on an unreasonably high pedestal  which is all to say that now I can  accept fallout 4 for what it is and not  what I want it to be I can sit back  relax and enjoy the way it pushes me  between  flourishing combat scavenging and  crafting I can take my time with the  workshop system expanding my factions  across the Commonwealth I can ignore the  main storyline in favor of finding my  own emergent anecdotes  well almost my biggest problem with  fallout 4 is the way Bethesda struggled  to tie its Golden Path and narrative  into its side quests whenever possible  I appreciate the team's attempts to do  so and it's a problem almost every open  world game shares but it feels as if  Bethesda tried to more fallout 4 into a  hardcore RPG in the 11th hour the story  asks you to take sides in a conflict  between the brotherhood of steel the  corporate Institute and the  aforementioned railroad balancing the  values and goals of each organization  for this playthrough I sided with the  railroad opting to help synths escape to  freedom whenever possible  I loved the Sutter fuge involved I love  how clandestine each mission feels I  love going behind the backs of the  Institute and the Brotherhood pulling  strings to steer matters toward our  righteous goal of liberty and justice  for all  the problem is each factions motivations  don't always clash in a logical way  often times I would make a decision sure  I was pissing off another group but upon  returning to its headquarters I was  welcomed with open arms in other cases I  would stand on the precipice of a  seemingly minor decision only to have  the game tell me I was about to ally  innate a faction entirely it was in  these moments that fallout 4 tried to  take too much control away from me the  player rather than showing me how my  actions would affect the outcome get  outed to tell me instead had Bethesda  found smoother ways to draw me toward  the main story beats and more intuitive  manners to pit various factions against  each other my enjoyment may have carried  into every aspect of this sandbox as it  stands now fallout 4 feels like two  separate games a very poor RPG and a  fantastic open-world shooter but when  the latter is so fantastic it's kind of  hard to complain there's exceptional  balance on display here in terms of  scope bgs is created not just an  engrossing world but an abundance of  amazing levels placed within that world  spending time in nuclear world's  amusement park reminded me of a metaphor  one of my editors used to use if you're  designing a theme park not only do you  have to select the best rollercoasters  possible you have to arrange them in a  pleasing way  in other words balance the pieces as  well as the path tying them all together  my editor was talking about sentence  structure and article flow but I think  it works here too it was 20 hours into  my playthrough I'm a southwest corner of  the map when I really knew fallout 4 had  nailed that balance I was in the glowing  sea where a new kit Massachusetts 200  years prior radiation levels were  critical supplies were scarce I was  vulnerable and I was having a blast I  came across a cave with power armor and  a few welcome supplies shortly before  wading into a thriving nest of lethal  radscorpions there were no RPG elements  or major story beats just a dangerous  world and the threat of death around  every corner  this was fallout 4 at its best I'll be  honest when I set out to replay fallout  4 I thought I would see it as I always  have a game whose reach far exceeds its  grasp a game with more ambition that can  handle resting in a sort of purgatory  before whatever comes next  [Music]  No  but now 75 hours and 5 DLCs later I  sincerely want to keep playing parts of  its design do feel very stagnant and it  doesn't do anything to revolutionize  open worlds and as with any bgs game it  is technically flawed to say the least  but in many ways it is the pinnacle of  what a fallout game can be a dangerous  world frozen in time whose every corner  is worth exploring  [Music]  they read the girl  [Music]
 

